---
pagination:
  data: stops
  size: 1
  alias: stop
permalink: "/stops/{{ stop.stopCode }}/tv/"
layout: layout.njk
styles: ["/css/tv.css"]
scripts: ["/js/tv.js"]
nav: false
footer: false
eleventyComputed:
  title: "{{ stop.stopName }} — TV board"
  description: "The next buses at stop {{ stop.stopCode }}, {{ stop.stopName }}, in large type for a screen on a wall."
---
{#
  The stop, for a television.

  A second view of a page that already exists, for a reader the stop page was
  never designed for: someone across a room who wants the next bus and cannot
  scroll, tap or zoom. So there is no menu, no footer, no save button and no
  links — nothing here is navigated to anything, because nobody is going to
  walk over and drive it. Point a screen at this URL and leave it.

  Unlike site/stops.njk this is not filled in by the worker. A stop page has to
  arrive with real times in it because a rider opens it, reads it and closes it
  inside ten seconds; a board is opened once and left running for a month, so
  the second it spends fetching its first snapshot costs nothing, and keeping
  it out of the snapshot writer keeps a second set of markers, and a second
  page written per stop per pass, out of src/lib/snapshots.ts. /js/tv.js does
  all of it from /data/arrivals/<code>.json.
#}
<main class="tv">
  <header class="tv-header">
    <h1 class="tv-stop">{{ stop.stopName }}</h1>
    {# Filled by /js/tv.js. Empty rather than server-rendered: a build-time
       clock on a page that runs for weeks would be wrong within a minute. #}
    <p class="tv-clock" id="tv-clock"></p>
  </header>

  {#
    The alert rail, tucked under the stop name and above the first arrival. The
    name stays pinned to the top — it is how a reader knows the board is about
    where they are standing — and the alert sits in the band beneath it, closed
    off by a line in its own colour. /js/tv.js fills it.
  #}
  <div id="tv-alert"></div>

  <p class="tv-stale" id="tv-stale" hidden></p>

  {#
    Three identifiers, because they answer three different questions: the code
    addresses the arrivals snapshot, and the GTFS id and route ids are what a
    service alert's selectors name. See /js/alert-rules.js.
  #}
  <div
    class="tv-board"
    id="tv-board"
    data-stop-code="{{ stop.stopCode }}"
    data-stop-id="{{ stop.stopId }}"
    data-route-ids="{{ stop.routes | join(',', 'routeId') }}"
  >
    <p class="tv-none">Loading…</p>
  </div>
</main>
